1:A nonsingular, anisotropic universe in a black holewith torsion and particle production Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Abstract References License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2007.11556v2 [gr-qc] 23 Jun 2026 General Relativity and Gravitation 53 (2), 18 (2021) A nonsingular, anisotropic universe in a black hole with torsion and particle production Nikodem J. Popławski Affiliation: NPoplawski@newhaven.edu Affiliation: Department of Mathematics and Physics, University of New Haven, West Haven, CT, USA Abstract We consider a universe formed in a black hole in general relativity with spin and torsion.
3:We use this metric to derive the Einstein–Cartan field equations with a relativistic spin fluid as a source.
4:We show that torsion may prevent a singularity and replace it with a nonsingular bounce if particle production dominates over shear.
5:Particle production after the last bounce can generate a finite period of inflation, during which the universe expands and isotropizes to the current state.
6:This scenario is only approximate: the Kantowski–Sachs metric is never reached and should be replaced with a more general metric that tends to that of a 3-sphere. Key words: Einstein–Cartan theory, torsion, spin fluid, black hole, Kantowski–Sachs metric, anisotropy, shear, particle production, bounce, nonsingular universe. 1. Introduction A recent evidence suggests that the universe may be closed [ 1 ] .
19:The multipole expansion of the conservation law for the spin tensor in EC gives a spin tensor which describes fermionic matter as a spin fluid (ideal fluid with spin) [ 11 ] . Hehl [ 12 ] , Trautman [ 13 ] , and Kopczyński [ 14 ] found that torsion can generate gravitational repulsion and prevent the formation of a cosmological singularity in a homogeneous and isotropic universe described by the Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) metric [ 15 , 6 , 16 , 17 ] when spins of fermions are aligned.
20:Hehl et al. [ 18 ] found that macroscopic averaging of the spin terms in the energy–momentum tensor gives a nonzero value even for randomly oriented spins.
21:Consequently, the effective energy density and pressure of a spin fluid are given by $\tilde{\epsilon}=\epsilon-\alpha n_{\textrm{f}}^{2},\quad\tilde{p}=p-\alpha n_{\textrm{f}}^{2},$ (1) where $\epsilon$ and $p$ are the thermodynamic energy density and pressure, $n_{\textrm{f}}$ is the number density of fermions, and $\alpha=\kappa(\hbar c)^{2}/32$ [ 5 , 18 , 19 , 20 ] .
24:Instead, the collapsing matter undergoes a nonsingular bounce [ 21 ] and then expands as a new, closed universe [ 4 , 5 , 22 ] whose total energy is zero [ 23 ] . Quantum particle production immediately after the bounce can generate a finite period of exponential inflation [ 5 ] that is consistent with the Planck 2015 observations of the cosmic microwave background radiation [ 24 ] .
29:Without further phenomena, such a universe would be singular and oscillatory [ 32 ] , and would not exceed the size of the parent black hole.
35:Further analysis was done in [ 43 ] for the Bianchi types with torsion, in [ 44 ] for some particular functions representing the scale factors, and in [ 45 ] for a spin fluid satisfying the equation of state of stiff matter. A closed, homogeneous, and anisotropic universe is either of the Bianchi type IX [ 16 ] or described by the KS metric.
36:In this article, we consider a KS anisotropic universe, formed in a black hole and filled with a spin fluid.
37:We investigate its dynamics if the spin fluid is created by quantum particle production [ 46 ] .
38:After reviewing the interior of a black hole, an anisotropic universe, and the shear tensor, we will demonstrate that such a universe can be nonsingular because of torsion and particle production acting together to violate the strong energy condition.
40:This scenario is only approximate: the exact KS metric is never reached in the presence of torsion and particle production and should be replaced with a more general form that allows the universe to tend to the closed FLRW geometry (a 3-sphere). 2. Black hole interior metric The interior of a black hole is given by the Schwarzschild metric: $ds^{2}=-\Bigl(\frac{2GM}{\rho}-1\Bigr)d\tau^{2}+\Bigl(\frac{2GM}{\rho}-1\Bigr)^{-1}d\rho^{2}-\rho^{2}(d\vartheta^{2}+\sin^{2}\vartheta\,d\varphi^{2}),$ (2) where $M$ is the mass of the black hole, $\tau$ is the external time coordinate, and $\rho<2GM$ is the external radial coordinate [ 16 ] .
52:Accordingly, the nonzero components of the energy–momentum tensor for a spin fluid, $T_{\mu\nu}=(\tilde{\epsilon}+\tilde{p})u_{\mu}u_{\nu}-\tilde{p}g_{\mu\nu}$ , are $T^{0}_{0}=\tilde{\epsilon}$ , $T^{1}_{1}=T^{2}_{2}=T^{3}_{3}=-\tilde{p}$ . The Einstein field equations $R^{\mu}_{\nu}=\kappa(T^{\mu}_{\nu}-1/2\,T\delta^{\mu}_{\nu})$ , where $R_{\mu\nu}=R^{\rho}_{\phantom{\rho}\mu\rho\nu}$ is the Ricci tensor and $\kappa=8\pi G$ , are therefore $\displaystyle R^{0}_{0}=-\frac{\ddot{X}}{X}-\frac{2\ddot{Y}}{Y}=\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde{\epsilon}+3\tilde{p}),$ $\displaystyle R^{1}_{1}=-\frac{\ddot{X}}{X}-\frac{2\dot{X}\dot{Y}}{XY}=\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde{p}-\tilde{\epsilon}),$ $\displaystyle R^{2}_{2}=R^{3}_{3}=-\frac{\ddot{Y}}{Y}-\frac{\dot{X}\dot{Y}}{XY}-\frac{\dot{Y}^{2}}{Y^{2}}-\frac{1}{Y^{2}}=\frac{\kappa}{2}(\tilde{p}-\tilde{\epsilon}),$ (9) where the dot denotes differentiation with respect to $t$ .
80:For a spin fluid, the last term in ( 31 ) is equal to $-\kappa(\tilde{\epsilon}+3\tilde{p})/2$ .
82:For a relativistic spin fluid, $p=\epsilon/3$ , this condition is equivalent to $2\kappa\alpha n_{\textrm{f}}^{2}>2\sigma^{2}+\kappa\epsilon.$ (32) Without torsion, the left-hand side of ( 32 ) would be absent and this inequality would not be satisfied, resulting in a singularity.
89:Consequently, torsion and particle production together can replace a singularity in a universe in a black hole with a nonsingular bounce. 8. Particle production The interior of a forming black hole would become a vacuum KS universe when all the matter in the black hole reached the central singularity.
90:However, quantum effects in changing gravitational fields cause particle-pair production that replaces vacuum with a relativistic spin fluid.
91:These effects may eliminate the singularity and replace it with a nonsingular bounce. The production rate of particles in a contracting or expanding universe can be phenomenologically given by $\frac{1}{\sqrt{-g}}\frac{d(\sqrt{-g}n_{\textrm{f}})}{dt}=\beta H^{4},$ (33) where $g=-X^{2}Y^{4}=-a^{6}$ is the determinant of the metric tensor in ( 6 ) and $\beta$ is the production rate [ 5 ] .
93:Comparing ( 34 ) with ( 30 ) gives $\frac{2\beta n_{\textrm{f}}}{81}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}+\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr)^{4}>\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl(\frac{\dot{X}}{X}-\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr).$ (35) After the formation of the event horizon, at the instant when ( 32 ) is reached, this inequality must be satisfied to ensure that ( 32 ) continues to hold. 9. Dynamics of universe with spin fluid The spin fluid in the early universe is formed by an ultrarelativistic matter in kinetic equilibrium, for which $\epsilon=h_{\star}T^{4}$ , $p=\epsilon/3$ , and $n_{\textrm{f}}=h_{n\textrm{f}}T^{3}$ , where $T$ is the temperature of the universe, $h_{\star}=(\pi^{2}/30)(g_{\textrm{b}}+(7/8)g_{\textrm{f}})k_{\textrm{B}}^{4}/(\hbar c)^{3}$ , and $h_{n\textrm{f}}=(\zeta(3)/\pi^{2})(3/4)g_{\textrm{f}}k_{\textrm{B}}^{3}/(\hbar c)^{3}$ [ 5 , 20 ] .
95:In the presence of spin and torsion, the first field equation in ( 10 ) with ( 1 ) becomes $\frac{\dot{Y}^{2}+1}{Y^{2}}+\frac{2\dot{X}\dot{Y}}{XY}=\kappa(h_{\star}T^{4}-\alpha h_{n\textrm{f}}^{2}T^{6}).$ (36) Without particle production, ( 1 ) and ( 14 ) give the constancy of $XY^{2}T^{3}$ , which is equivalent to $\dot{T}/T+H=0$ .
96:With particle production, this constancy is replaced with a relation that follows from the first equation in ( 34 ): $\frac{\dot{T}}{T}+H=\frac{\beta H^{4}}{3h_{n\textrm{f}}T^{3}}.$ (37) Equations ( 36 ) and ( 37 ), with ( 11 ) and ( 16 ), determine the time dependence of $X$ , $Y$ , and $T$ .
97:The only parameters are the mass $M$ of the parent black hole that created the universe and the production rate $\beta$ .
102:The interior of a Kerr black hole would not be represented by the KS metric; it would be a more general, anisotropic universe. If $\beta$ is big enough, then particle production will increase $T$ quickly enough to make the right-hand side of ( 36 ) negative while keeping the left-hand side positive.
106:Because of particle production, which increases entropy, each cycle of expansion and contraction lasts longer and reaches a larger maximum of $Y$ than the preceding cycle [ 5 , 33 ] .
111:If the production rate $\beta$ is too small, then particle production might be insufficient to prevent a cosmological singularity.
114:Consequently, there is a range of the production rate that prevents a singularity and does not cause eternal inflation. If the right-hand side of ( 38 ) is slightly lesser than 1, then $T$ would be approximately constant.
125:We analyzed a closed Kantowski–Sachs universe satisfying the Einstein–Cartan field equations with a spin fluid.
126:We showed that torsion and particle production may together violate the strong energy condition and give the universe one or more nonsingular bounces.
127:Furthermore, particle production in an expanding phase can generate a finite period of inflation, during which the universe produces enormous amounts of matter and entropy, and isotropizes. This scenario is only approximate.
128:Its purpose was to show how torsion and particle production could avoid a singularity even if the shear is present.

